<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Catherine Jagiellon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1526–1583</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Katarzyna Jagiellonka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Catherine Jagiellon was a Polish princess, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-i-the-old">Sigismund I the Old</a>, who in 1562 married Duke John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-of-sweden">John III of Sweden</a>. When his brother Eric XIV imprisoned John at Gripsholm in 1563, she chose to share his captivity. As Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> from 1569 she supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, and her son became King Sigismund III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and Sweden.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/catherine-jagiellon.jpg" alt="Catherine Jagiellon" width="320" height="376" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lucas Cranach the Younger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cranach_the_Younger_Catherine_Jagiellon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
